    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Kimi Räikkönen is a Finnish racing driver who won the 2007 Formula One world championship with Ferrari. Known as the Iceman, he earned his fortune through racing contracts and endorsements. His career brought twenty-one Grand Prix victories, spells with five Formula One teams and two seasons in the World Rally Championship.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Kimi Räikkönen's parents made practical sacrifices to keep him racing karts. His father, a road builder, took extra work driving taxis and working as a nightclub bouncer, while his mother worked as an office clerk. Money earmarked for a bathroom in their Finnish home went towards racing instead.\n\nHe had competed in just twenty-three car races when manager David Robertson persuaded Peter Sauber to offer him a Formula One test. At Mugello, Räikkönen quickly identified that the car needed more front grip. Changes to the front wing brought an immediate improvement, and his speed and consistency persuaded Sauber to give him a race seat.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Primary biography and participant oral history support formation, named provider of opportunity and concrete test mechanism."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 104,
    "html": "<p>Kimi Räikkönen's parents made practical sacrifices to keep him racing karts. His father, a road builder, took extra work driving taxis and working as a nightclub bouncer, while his mother worked as an office clerk. Money earmarked for a bathroom in their Finnish home went towards racing instead.</p>\r\n<p>He had competed in just twenty-three car races when manager David Robertson persuaded Peter Sauber to offer him a Formula One test. At Mugello, Räikkönen quickly identified that the car needed more front grip. Changes to the front wing brought an immediate improvement, and his speed and consistency persuaded Sauber to give him a race seat.</p>\r\n"
  },
